proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At the initial open-hand approach in a task requiring two repetitions, the remembered front-image pixel correctly identified the demonstrated blue cube among several blocks, but the chosen approach was displaced in world x by more than 1 cm. Target identity and repetition history were not missing; precise localization failed the waypoint SUFFICIENCY criterion. Strengthen the initial-approach localization rule: target_location selects the physical cube only and is not a precision grasp coordinate. Before commanding the z=0.15 approach, refine that cube's top-face center from the current images. Use the center of the top-face polygon, excluding visible side faces, rather than the center of the whole colored silhouette. Back-project that center using the supplied calibration onto the approximately z=0.04 top-face plane. Independently localize the same cube in the current calibrated wrist view and cross-check the resulting world xy; resolve discrepancies using the clearly visible top-face boundaries before issuing the approach. Use the refined world xy for the approach and derive yaw from the cube edges. This makes the existing general visual-localization requirement explicit for the first approach; it does not add a memory field, an episode-specific coordinate correction, or an extra realignment stage."
}

